false_positives.candidate:
try/assertThat/fail/catch pattern is misleading and contains dead `fail()` call

The assertion
  try {
      MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
      fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
  } catch (Exception nsee) {
      // expected
  }
is repeated (around lines 291-296 and 312-317). It works only by accident: if the element is present, Hamcrest's assertThat throws AssertionError (an Error, not an Exception), which is NOT caught by `catch (Exception nsee)`, so the test correctly fails. If the element is absent, NoSuchElementException is thrown and is caught. The follow-up `fail()` call is unreachable, and catching `Exception` (rather than `NoSuchElementException`) hides other WebDriver failures as a silent pass. The other test in this directory (`PasskeysUsernameFormTest.passwordLoginWithNonDiscoverableKey` at line 202) already uses the right idiom: `Assert.assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']") ));`. Use the same here.

true_positives.matched_candidate:
join() may leave later processes alive when timeout is hit early

In SpanFlusher.join(), the deadline check at the top of the for-loop over self.processes is:

    for process_index, process in self.processes.items():
        if deadline is not None:
            remaining_time = deadline - time.time()
            if remaining_time <= 0:
                break

When self.next_step.join(timeout) consumes most of the timeout (which is common because it is called first), the deadline check can break out of the loop on the second or later iteration without ever calling process.terminate() on the remaining processes. Those subprocesses only have the shared `stopped` flag, so if any of them is stuck (e.g., blocked in a Redis call, Kafka send, or sleep) they will linger as zombie processes because nothing in the loop forces a SIGTERM. With the new multi-process design this affects more than one process. The fix is to drop the early `break` and let the inner `while` condition plus the per-process `process.terminate()` handle the deadline uniformly for every entry in self.processes.

false_positives.candidate:
isProvisionedPromRule returns false for non-Grafana prom rules via incorrect truthy coercion

`isProvisionedPromRule(promRule)` is defined as:

```
export function isProvisionedPromRule(promRule: PromRuleDTO): boolean {
  return prometheusRuleType.grafana.rule(promRule) && Boolean(promRule.provenance);
}
```

When passed a Cloud/Mimir `PromRuleDTO` (which has no `folderUid`/`uid`, hence `prometheusRuleType.grafana.rule` returns false), the function returns the falsy `false` rather than `false` via the type guard - that is fine, but it can be misleading because a missing `provenance` for a non-Grafana rule will return `false` as expected, yet a missing `provenance` for a Grafana rule returns `false` without distinguishing between 'not provisioned' and 'rule is not Grafana at all'. More importantly, the consumer `getIsProvisioned` in `RuleActionsButtons.V2.tsx` calls this even when the caller has not verified the rule is a Grafana rule; if a future caller passes a non-Grafana `promRule` (e.g. Mimir) the function silently returns false without indicating the rule was unhandled. Not a hard bug today, but worth flagging because the silent-false contract differs from `isProvisionedRule` (which type-narrows via `isGrafanaRulerRule`).

